 @partahauki1  1. 380 didn't compete with the 960 - the 280X did. And the 280X was already 3 years old at the time (7970Ghz rebranded) and it cost 30% less than the 960, yet was 20% faster. Even Nvidias own 770 was faster than the 960.
2. The 390 didn't compete with the 970, the 290 did. And sure, the 970 was some 15% faster than the 290 at stock. BUT that is why AMD reduced their prices of the 290/290X overnight, from £330/£450 respectively to an insane £230/£350. Meaning that the 290 was now 35% less expensive than a 970 (but just 15% slower) and the 290 was just 5% more expensive (but 10% faster).
3. Within 2 years of the 970 being released, Nvidia dropped all but critical driver support for Maxwell. This, coupled with the fact that Maxwell / Pascal could only emulate vulkan / DX12 extensions in software, meant the 290 not only beat the 970 by then, but also beat the 980. Meaning a £230 (R9 290) beat the £450 card (GTX980) just two years after it launched.
